Depends on the "Split committed work" section from the
interactive-staging topic (cc/interactive-staging-split); merge that
first.

Tested in throwaway repos with a target commit that edits f1 in two
places and adds n1 and n2, and a child that edits f1 and n1. Split:
the stop ends in two commits, `git diff <orig> HEAD` is empty, and
the rebase finishes with the original tip's tree. Removal (keep hunk 1
and n1): after `git restore --staged --worktree -- :/` the break stop
shows exactly the dropped hunk and n2, and the rebase finishes.

What does not work, from the same tests: `git restore -- .` and
`git checkout -- .` keep the intent-to-add entry, and
`git rebase --continue` refuses with a merge-conflict message although
there is no conflict. `git rm --cached` removes the entry but leaves
the file untracked; when a later commit touches it, the pick fails
with "untracked working tree files would be overwritten by merge".
The plan expected `git rm --cached` to be the answer; it is only half
of it.

Assumed, not sourced: `git reset --hard` discards the same leftovers
and also worked. I used restore so the command names what it discards
and does not touch HEAD; that is my preference, not something the
tests decide.
